<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Advice for a Ninth Grader Is...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Write a review of a recent event, place, or product. Interactive: Write collaborative stories or articles.</p>
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write a review of a study app or tool, describing its features and usefulness for academic success (e.g., "This app is great for organizing notes. It helps you stay focused and manage your time better.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can collaborate with classmates to write an article on effective study habits, sharing personal experiences and advice (e.g., "Otto suggested that if I studied earlier, I wouldn't feel so rushed before exams. He recommends setting daily study goals to avoid last-minute cramming.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can write a short review of a recent school event, highlighting its impact on academic skills (e.g., "The study skills workshop was helpful. We learned how to take better notes and manage time effectively.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Warm-up:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are shown a picture of a 9th-grade classroom and asked to brainstorm challenges that ninth graders might face in terms of academic success (e.g., "time management," "procrastination," "finding motivation"). The teacher writes their ideas on the board, eliciting key vocabulary like <i>effort, focus, discipline, plan, strategy</i>.</p>
                    <b>Modeling:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models giving advice using second conditional and modals. For example, "If I <i>were</i> a ninth grader, I <i>would create</i> a study schedule." "You <i>should</i> develop good time management skills." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key vocabulary, focusing on the stress patterns of two-syllable nouns and verbs with identical spelling (e.g., "PROgress" as a noun, "proGRESS" as a verb) and contrastive sounds (e.g., /gr/ in "growth" and /kr/ in "create").</p>
                    <b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher asks:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'If I studied harder, I would get better grades,' am I talking about a real or imaginary situation?" (Expected answer: Imaginary/Hypothetical)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'You should focus on your goals' mean? Is it a strong command or a recommendation?" (Expected answer: A recommendation/good advice)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Material:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A short informal article or blog post titled "Top 5 Tips for Ninth Graders to Achieve Academic Success," written in an encouraging and direct tone. The text includes examples of second conditional, modals for advice, and first conditional, using target vocabulary (e.g., <i>achievement, strategy, motivation, time management, improve, organize, recommend, consistent</i>).</p>
                    <b>Communicative Competence:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can collaborate with classmates to write an article on effective study habits, sharing personal experiences and advice.</p>
                    <b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this article?" (Expected answer: To give advice to ninth graders for academic success.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match each of the "Top 5 Tips" from the article to a specific academic skill it helps develop (e.g., "Creating a study plan" matches "time management").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify key phrases:</b> Students underline all sentences in the article that use second conditional, modals for advice, or first conditional.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Practice writing advice using second conditional (If I were..., I would...), modals for advice (You should/must/can...), and first conditional (If you..., you will...) with target vocabulary related to academic achievement. Emphasis on correct sentence structure and appropriate word choice.</p>
                    <b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap Fill & Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences with appropriate verbs or modals to form advice related to academic success. For example: "If I _____ (be) a ninth grader, I _____ (organize) my notes daily." or "You _____ (focus) on one task at a time to avoid procrastination." "If you _____ (practice) your English regularly, you _____ (develop) better fluency." They then write 2-3 original sentences following these patterns.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Advice Rephrasing:</b> In pairs, students receive simple advice statements (e.g., "Study more for exams."). They must rephrase this advice using either the second conditional, a modal, or the first conditional, incorporating at least one new vocabulary word. For instance, "If you want better performance, you should improve your study strategy." or "If you reflect on your progress, you will achieve your goals." They write down their rephrased sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Building & Peer Check:</b> Students work in small groups of three. Each student writes an incomplete sentence of advice (e.g., "If I were more disciplined, I..."). They pass their sentence to the right, and the next student completes it. Then, the third student adds a follow-up piece of advice using a different grammatical structure (e.g., "If I were more disciplined, I would track my study hours. You should also set achievable goals."). Groups then peer-check each other's sentences for correct grammar, vocabulary use (e.g., <i>skill, development, effort</i>), and clarity, focusing on written accuracy.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Task Format:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Collaborative writing of an article section. Students will work in pairs to write one paragraph (approx. 80-100 words) of an article titled "Our Best Advice for Academic Success in Ninth Grade."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each pair receives a specific sub-topic related to academic success (e.g., "Time Management," "Study Habits," "Motivation and Goal Setting," "Seeking Feedback").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using the grammar structures (second conditional, modals for advice, first conditional) and vocabulary (e.g., <i>achievement, development, strategy, focus, progress, recommend</i>) practiced, each pair drafts a paragraph providing actionable advice to a ninth grader on their assigned sub-topic. They should share personal experiences or hypothetical situations to make the advice relevant. For example, "If I were a ninth grader, I would plan my week ahead to manage my time better. You should prioritize your assignments to avoid last-minute cramming."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support, encouragement, and scaffolding (e.g., "What advice *would* you give if they *struggled* with motivation?" or "What *should* a ninth grader do to *improve* their grades?"). The teacher encourages students to use inclusive language (e.g., "Let's consider...", "We can all benefit from...").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs then exchange their drafted paragraphs with another pair for a quick peer review, checking for clarity and completeness of advice, as well as appropriate use of target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students submit their collaboratively written paragraph for the article "Our Best Advice for Academic Success in Ninth Grade."</p>
                    <b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar (Second Conditional, Modals, First Conditional):</b> Accurately applies at least two of the three target grammatical structures (e.g., "If I were more organized, I would...", "You should...", "If you focus, you will...").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates a minimum of 5-7 relevant vocabulary words from the list (e.g., <i>time management, strategy, achievement, improve, motivate, recommend, plan</i>).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Actionable Advice:</b> The advice is clear, easy to understand, and practical for a ninth grader.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Cohesion:</b> The paragraph demonstrates evidence of collaboration and presents ideas in a coherent, organized manner.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Writing Mechanics:</b> Minor errors in spelling, punctuation, and sentence structure (B1.1 level expectation).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new piece of advice you learned how to write effectively today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel using the second conditional to give advice in writing?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student self-reflection and teacher reflection)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which part of writing the advice article was most challenging for you and why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies or grammar points will you focus on to improve your academic writing in the future?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The collaborative writing task worked well, encouraging peer interaction and the application of diverse grammar structures. Students showed good understanding of the second conditional and modals for advice. Some pairs struggled with integrating the first conditional smoothly into their paragraphs, and some vocabulary items (e.g., 'facilitate,' 'procrastinate') required extra scaffolding.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Adjustments for next time: Provide more sentence starters or partial frames for the first conditional to aid integration. Offer a wider range of sub-topics to allow for more diverse advice. Encourage a stronger focus on the distinction between PROgress (noun) and proGRESS (verb) when giving feedback on written work.</p>
                    <b>Connection to next lesson:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The advice paragraphs written today will serve as input for Lesson 5, where students will mediate and integrate this information to create a comprehensive digital "Ninth Grade Survival Guide" as part of their 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>